{#
  The evaluator review banner, shown at the top of an evaluation.

  Expects:
    tally    — {agree, disagree, total} from EvaluationReviewRepository::tallyFor
    myReview — the current user's own EvaluationReview, or null
    can_review — whether this user may record one (drives the "add yours" link)

  ── Why this sits UNDER the <h1> and not above it ──────────────────────────
  "At the top" and "before the heading" are not the same thing. A section
  heading that appears before the page's <h1> makes the document outline start
  at level 2 and jump backwards, which is exactly the pattern screen reader
  users navigating by heading rely on being sensible. Placed immediately after
  the <h1> it is still the first thing on the page after the title — visually
  the top — while the outline stays h1 → h2.

  ── Why the numbers are text and not a chart ───────────────────────────────
  Same reasoning as the match-count tiles further down the page: two integers
  and a total do not need an axis. A stacked bar here would also imply a
  proportion that three reviews cannot support.
#}
{% if is_granted(constant('Pixiekat\\LuminaUIBundle\\Interfaces\\Security\\Voter\\GenericVoterInterface::CAN_VIEW_EVALUATION_SUMMARY')) %}
  <section class="review-summary" aria-labelledby="review-summary-heading">
    <h2 id="review-summary-heading">Evaluator review</h2>

    {% if tally.total == 0 %}
      <p class="muted">
        No evaluator has reviewed this evaluation yet.
        {% if can_review %}<a href="#review">Be the first</a>.{% endif %}
      </p>
    {% else %}
      {#
        One sentence first, then the badges. The sentence is what a screen reader
        reads out as a unit and what someone skimming actually needs; the badges
        repeat it for people scanning visually. Neither carries meaning the other
        lacks — the counts are spelled out in both.
      #}
      {% if myReview %}
        <p class="review-tally">
          <strong>{{ tally.total }}</strong> evaluator{{ tally.total == 1 ? '' : 's' }}
          reviewed this evaluation:
          <span class="badge badge--agree">{{ tally.agree }} agree</span>
          <span class="badge badge--disagree">{{ tally.disagree }} disagree</span>
        </p>
      {% else %}
        <p class="text-muted">Submit a review to see how other evaluators have weighed in.</p>
      {% endif %}
    {% endif %}

    {#
      Your own verdict, echoed back. Worth its own line rather than being folded
      into the tally: "2 agree" does not tell you whether you were one of them,
      and the most common reason to reopen an evaluation you have already reviewed
      is to check or change what you said.
    #}
    {% if myReview %}
      <p class="review-mine">
        <span class="badge badge--{{ myReview.verdict.value }}">You: {{ myReview.verdict.shortLabel }}</span>
        {% if myReview.reasonLabel %}
          <span class="muted">— {{ myReview.reasonLabel }}</span>
        {% endif %}
        {% if is_granted(constant('Pixiekat\\LuminaUIBundle\\Interfaces\\Security\\Voter\\GenericVoterInterface::CAN_EDIT_EVALUATION_REVIEW')) %}
          <a href="#review">Change your review</a>
        {% endif %}
      </p>
    {% elseif can_review and tally.total > 0 %}
      <p class="review-mine">
        <a href="#review">Record your own review</a>
      </p>
    {% endif %}
  </section>
{% endif %}
